galle1@husc7.harvard.edu (Brian Galle) writes:

>1. Enchant armor scrolls enchant a random piece of armor. Wrong. They enchant
> the outermost piece (cloak before armor before Hawaiian shirt, for instance.)

When you have multiple "outermost" armour pieces, e.g. a cloak, a
helmet and boots, a random one is picked.

>2. Enchanting a +3 piece of armor will dissolve it. Again wrong. The odds
> of detroying armor, unless I'm much mistaken, are 1/(the current bonus of
> the armor). I'm not sure what happens if the armor has a negative bonus, or
> if what I've said is true for +2 armor (but I think it is).

*Completely* wrong. This would mean that the chance of destroying a +1
armour would be 1 (100%)! Also, the chance of destroying an armour
would *decrease* when the bonus goes up.

In NH3.0, the "rules" were as follows (quoting from the old spoilers,
the rules might have changed):

Your chance for succeeding (and not causing the item to disintegrate
due to over enchantment) is totally dependent upon the current bonus of
the item. If the current bonus is three or less (five or less for
elven mithril coats), you will always succeed. Otherwise, you have one
chance out of whatever the current bonus of the item is. In other
words, if the bonus is +4, you have a 1/4 or 25% chance of being
successful. If the bonus is +8, you have a 1/8 or 12.5% chance of
being successful. Thus, it is theoretically possible to enchant armor
up to +maxint, but the laws of probability would not favor it!

These "rules" might have changed after NH 3.0.

